    id: "landmark-clever-hans", title: "Clever Hans, the counting horse", domain: "research",
    story: "In Berlin in the early 1900s, a horse called Hans seemed to answer arithmetic questions by tapping his hoof. In 1907 the psychologist Oskar Pfungst showed that Hans could answer only when the questioner knew the answer and was visible. The horse was reading tiny, unintended changes in posture and expression that signalled when to stop tapping.",
    believedThen: "That an animal could do arithmetic, because experts watching saw no trickery.",
