    id: "landmark-hofling", title: "Hofling's hospital obedience study", domain: "social",
    story: "In 1966 Charles Hofling arranged for an unknown 'doctor' to phone 22 night nurses in US hospitals and order them to give twice the maximum dose of an unfamiliar drug, written on the bottle, before he arrived. Hospital rules forbade phone orders from unknown doctors. Twenty-one of the 22 nurses were stopped on their way to give the drug, which was actually a harmless placebo.",
